<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phoenicia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phoenicia was the ancient coastal region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, roughly modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> with parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and northern Israel, home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> and Arwad. From about 1200 BCE its merchants and sailors traded across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, founded colonies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and spread their alphabet. The region later fell under Assyrian, Persian, Hellenistic and Roman rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phoenicia.jpg" alt="Phoenicia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kordas, based on Alvaro's work · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phoenicia_map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>But in the Iron Age, commercial and political leadership in <b>Phoenicia</b> passed to Tyre and Sidon, whose fleets and colonies spread across the Mediterranean, while Byblos remained a smaller port.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The city’s workshops produced purple-dyed textiles using the murex process inherited from <b>Phoenicia</b>, fine metalwork, glass and ceramics that commanded high prices across Mediterranean markets.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…may have been the oldest; from it and the other ports a great Mediterranean commercial empire was founded, and tradition held that a colonising party from Sidon went out to found Tyre, which grew into a rival, each claiming to be the metropolis, the mother city, of <b>Phoenicia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>An earthquake of estimated magnitude 7.5 struck Byzantine <b>Phoenicia</b>, and the tsunami it triggered brought great destruction to the province's coastal towns and sank many ships.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-9">9 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It also had close links with <b>Phoenicia</b>, and it was probably among the Cypriot kingdoms that paid tribute to Assyria in the 7th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-amathus">Kingdom of Amathus</a></li><li><blockquote>Their best-known leader is Mavia, who around 378 led a revolt that ravaged Palestine and <b>Phoenicia</b> and ended only when Valens agreed to her demand for an orthodox Arab hermit, Moses, as bishop of her people.</blockquote><a href="/state/tanukhids">Tanukhids</a></li></ul></section>
